A||||4||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||2.5L 4cyl Head Gasket Replacement||||5||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||12-23-2000||09:26 PM||||I have water droplets comming out of the exhaust of my 2.5L 4cyl.  The coolant is blackish green.  The idle is a bit uneven.<P>This engine was professionally rebuilt about a year ago.  There are bolts missing from the alternator mount, and other signs of incompetence galore.<P>This will be the first head gasket I have done on a 2.5L Duke.  I have been told you must replace the head bolts.  Is this true?<P>Who sells the head bolt set for a 1984 2.5L 4 cylinder?<P>What mistakes are commonly made when installing a head gasket in this type of engine?  It seems like the head bolts get torqued pretty hard.   ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||12-23-2000||09:44 PM||||I would strongly recommend getting a Haynes manual to help with the head gasket replacement.  It should have pretty good step by step procedures for removal and assembly.<P>The Fiero head bolts are usually torque to yield.  They should NEVER be reused.  The manual will provide specific torques values and the correct sequence.<P>You should be able to get a set of head bolts from any decent auto parts store.  NAPA or Carquest stores should be able to fix you up.  <P>When replacing the valve cover gasket, you can get either cork or rubber gaskets.  I have heard that the rubber seals better than the cork.  I think FelPro makes a rubber gasket that I got at AutoZone.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||12-24-2000||11:20 AM||||The rubber valve cover gaskets are much better. I'll never use a cork one unless it's all I can get.<P>I agree about replacing the head bolts. The book doesn't say so but they have the same install procedure as TTY so I would treat them as such.<P>You'll want to borow,rent,whatever a dial for this. It's a protractor mounted on a socket extension. It has a little magnetic based pointer. You use this to accurately do the anlge torque sequence. Also, you use a breaker bar or sockwet hanle and <B>not</B> the torque wrench for the angle torque steps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||12-24-2000||12:53 PM||||You may want to shop around for head bolts...they are a little pricey.  I don't know about the 4-cyl, but V6 head bolts (Fel-Pro brand) ranged from about $25 to $34 for the complete set. AutoZone was the lowest price.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||chrissherman||12-25-2000||12:41 AM||||Head boolts are $14.00 at napa in alaska also use the coppergasket spray in the can. Grate stuff||||reg||1
